    Why: gcc's -flto can only inline across GIMPLE it produced, and a Rust staticlib's
    archive members are native objects. So R5's C++ hazard does not bite a C++ host
    over THIS core. That is a fact about the toolchain pair, reported rather than
    assumed, and it would not hold for a core compiled by the same LTO.

== half two: is the no-boundary control fused into the benchmark loop? ==

  C18. This used to ask whether the control function was LARGER than the largest
  timing closure in the image and take that as evidence it was not copied into one.
  Size is a proxy for fusion, not a test of it, and its positive control was -flto,
  which fires only when the optimiser happens to fuse something. After the core moved
  (W10) the largest closure went from 1433 B to 911 B, the 1155 B control landed on
  the other side of the line, and the control went quiet without anyone deciding it
  should. A size-threshold control is fragile by construction.

  Two direct properties instead, and a fixture that proves the checker can report
  both. The control is reached through a FUNCTION POINTER handed to the case runner,
  so there is no direct call site to count -- what fusion would destroy is (1) the
  out-of-line body and (2) the call instruction in the closure.

  the checker's own control (src/fusion_probe.cpp): one function that MUST be called
  and one that MUST be fused, both by construction rather than by optimisation level
  [FAIL] fusion_probe is not built, so half two has no control and proves nothing

  The control is reached through a FUNCTION POINTER passed to the case runner, so its
  address is taken and an out-of-line body must exist. Both properties above are the
  question itself rather than a proxy for it, and the fixture says the checker can
  report either answer.

